023"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "outliers", "eyebrow": "Read it right", "title": "Averages hide long tails", "body": "Mandiant left out 15 outliers. With them, the 2023 average rises from five days to 47.", "sources": [{"url": "https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/threat-intelligence/time-to-exploit-trends-2023?hl=en", "label": "Google Cloud (Mandiant), time-to-exploit trends 2023"}]}, {"kind": "numberSense", "id": "ns-tte", "prompt": "What was the average time-to-exploit in 2023, in days?", "min": 0, "max": 40, "value": 5, "unit": "days", "tolerance": 0.06, "explanation": "Five days, down from 32 in 2021 and 2022 and 63 in 2018 and 2019."}, {"kind": "multipleChoice", "id": "mc", "prompt": "What does a five-day time-to-exploit mean for a monthly patch cycle?", "options": ["It is fast enough for most flaws", "Attackers can strike weeks before it", "It no longer matters at all"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "If attacks start within days, waiting up to a month leaves systems open."}, {"id": "lens-curiosity", "kind": "info", "title": "From two months to five days", "body": "Attackers once took about two months to use a new flaw. By 2023, it was five days on average.", "eyebrow": "Why it matters", "sources": [{"url": "https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/threat-intelligence/time-to-exploit-trends-2023?hl=en", "label": "Google Cloud (Mandiant), time-to-exploit trends 2023"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In cyber, defenders are racing a clock that keeps getting shorter.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/threat-intelligence/time-to-exploit-trends-2023?hl=en", "label": "Google Cloud (Mandiant), time-to-exploit trends 2023"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "Average time-to-exploit fell from 63 days in 2018 and 2019 to five days in 2023, and about 70% of exploited flaws were zero-days.", "sectionKey": "numbers"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$4ef6b9b9-5990-5c41-bd59-5eb34ab62c12$ml$::uuid, $ml$cybersecurity$ml$, $ml$The flaw hiding in everything$ml$, 'MICRO_LESSON', ARRAY[$ml$MICRO_LESSON$ml$, $ml$day-11$ml$, $ml$month-1$ml$, $ml$handwritten-v1$ml$, $ml$goal:career$ml$, $ml$level:beginner$ml$, $ml$level:intermediate$ml$, $ml$level:advanced$ml$]::text[], 5, now(), '{}'::jsonb, $ml${"id": "cybersecurity-d11-career", "title": "The flaw hiding in everything", "exercises": [{"id": "open", "kind": "coldOpen", "kicker": "One small library, inside countless products.", "eyebrow": "Day 11 · The risk", "headline": "In July 2022, a US review board called the Log4j flaw an endemic vulnerability.", "fullText": "Log4j is a free logging library used across huge amounts of software. Its flaw, found by Alibaba in late 2021, let attackers take over systems. The Cyber Safety Review Board warned that unpatched versions will remain in systems for the next decade, if not longer, and said open-source developers are thinly resourced.", "sources": [{"url": "https://therecord.media/first-cyber-safety-review-board-report-finds-log4j-has-become-an-endemic-vulnerability/", "label": "The Record, Log4j is an endemic vulnerability"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "hidden", "eyebrow": "The problem", "text": "Companies often did not know Log4j was inside their products.", "highlight": "You cannot patch what you do not know you run.", "sources": [{"url": "https://therecord.media/first-cyber-safety-review-board-report-finds-log4j-has-become-an-endemic-vulnerability/", "label": "The Record, Log4j is an endemic vulnerability"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "endemic", "eyebrow": "The verdict", "text": "\"This event is not over. The risk remains,\" the board's chair said.", "highlight": "The board made 19 recommendations.", "sources": [{"url": "https://therecord.media/first-cyber-safety-review-board-report-finds-log4j-has-become-an-endemic-vulnerability/", "label": "The Record, Log4j is an endemic vulnerability"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "open-source", "eyebrow": "The root cause", "title": "Free code, thin support", "body": "Critical software is often kept up by a few volunteers. The board called for better security practices in open source.", "sources": [{"url": "https://therecord.media/first-cyber-safety-review-board-report-finds-log4j-has-become-an-endemic-vulnerability/", "label": "The Record, Log4j is an endemic vulnerability"}]}, {"kind": "multipleChoice", "id": "mc", "prompt": "Why did the board call Log4j \"endemic\"?", "options": ["It was only found in Chinese systems", "Unpatched copies will linger for years", "It could not be fixed by any patch"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A fix exists, but the library is buried in so many products that old copies will stay for a decade or more."}, {"kind": "theCall", "id": "call", "situation": "A flaw like Log4j is announced. You are not sure where the library is used in your company.", "prompt": "What do you do first?", "options": ["Patch only the systems you remember", "Find every place the library is used", "Wait for vendors to tell you"], "consequences": ["Forgotten systems stay open.", "You cannot patch what you have not found.", "Attackers will not wait for vendors."], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "An inventory of what software you run comes before any patch."}, {"id": "lens-career", "kind": "info", "title": "Know your dependencies", "body": "Log4j showed teams often did not know what was inside their software. Inventory skills are in demand.", "eyebrow": "For your interview", "sources": [{"url": "https://therecord.media/first-cyber-safety-review-board-report-finds-log4j-has-become-an-endemic-vulnerability/", "label": "The Record, Log4j is an endemic vulnerability"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In cyber, the risk you inherit from other people's code is still your risk.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://therecord.media/first-cyber-safety-review-board-report-finds-log4j-has-become-an-endemic-vulnerability/", "label": "The Record, Log4j is an endemic vulnerability"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "Log4j, a free library inside countless products, had a flaw a US board called endemic in 2022. Unpatched copies may linger for a decade or more.", "sectionKey": "risk"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
