 as financially motivated and native English-speaking, which makes their calls convincing.", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.adaptivesecurity.com/blog/mgm-ransomware-attack", "label": "Adaptive Security, the MGM attack, citing MGM filings"}]}, {"kind": "faceOff", "id": "face", "prompt": "Who holds more power over an employee's account?", "left": {"name": "The help desk", "note": "Can reset passwords and MFA"}, "right": {"name": "The firewall", "note": "Filters network traffic"}, "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "A reset hands over the account. No firewall stops a legitimate login."}, {"kind": "multipleChoice", "id": "mc", "prompt": "What cut the MGM attackers' path short at other firms?", "options": ["A longer password policy", "Verifying callers before any reset", "A faster internet connection"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "If the help desk verifies identity properly, a convincing voice is not enough."}, {"id": "lens-build_startup", "kind": "info", "title": "Verify the caller", "body": "Help desks need fast, reliable ways to confirm who is calling. That is a product.", "eyebrow": "For founders", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.adaptivesecurity.com/blog/mgm-ransomware-attack", "label": "Adaptive Security, the MGM attack, citing MGM filings"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In cyber, whoever can reset access holds the real power.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.adaptivesecurity.com/blog/mgm-ransomware-attack", "label": "Adaptive Security, the MGM attack, citing MGM filings"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "The 2023 MGM attack cost about $100 million. Attackers claimed a call to the IT help desk got them in, showing the power of whoever can reset access.", "sectionKey": "who_hurts"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$da514d19-1c54-5f19-aee8-3ee3fad2d70b$ml$::uuid, $ml$cybersecurity$ml$, $ml$The help desk holds the keys$ml$, 'MICRO_LESSON', ARRAY[$ml$MICRO_LESSON$ml$, $ml$day-21$ml$, $ml$month-1$ml$, $ml$handwritten-v1$ml$, $ml$goal:curiosity$ml$, $ml$level:beginner$ml$, $ml$level:intermediate$ml$, $ml$level:advanced$ml$]::text[], 5, now(), '{}'::jsonb, $ml${"id": "cybersecurity-d21-curiosity", "title": "The help desk holds the keys", "exercises": [{"id": "open", "kind": "coldOpen", "kicker": "A phone call, not malware.", "eyebrow": "Day 21 · The power", "headline": "In September 2023, a cyberattack disrupted MGM Resorts for about 10 days and cost it about $100 million.", "fullText": "MGM's filing put the hit to its adjusted property earnings at about $100 million, plus under $10 million in one-time costs. MGM never named the way in. The attackers, linked to the group known as Scattered Spider, claimed they found an employee online and called the IT help desk posing as them.", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.adaptivesecurity.com/blog/mgm-ransomware-attack", "label": "Adaptive Security, the MGM attack, citing MGM filings"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "reset", "eyebrow": "The weak point", "text": "Whoever can reset a password holds power over every account.", "highlight": "Help desks exist to say yes to stressed callers.", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.adaptivesecurity.com/blog/mgm-ransomware-attack", "label": "Adaptive Security, the MGM attack, citing MGM filings"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "claim", "eyebrow": "The claim", "text": "The attackers said the call took about 10 minutes.", "highlight": "That account has not been confirmed by investigators.", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.adaptivesecurity.com/blog/mgm-ransomware-attack", "label": "Adaptive Security, the MGM attack, citing MGM filings"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "group", "eyebrow": "The attackers", "title": "English-speaking social engineers", "body": "Microsoft describes Scattered Spider as financially motivated and native English-speaking, which makes their calls convincing.", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.adaptivesecurity.com/blog/mgm-ransomware-attack", "label": "Adaptive Security, the MGM attack, citing MGM filings"}]}, {"kind": "faceOff", "id": "face", "prompt": "Who holds more power over an employee's account?", "left": {"name": "The help desk", "note": "Can reset passwords and MFA"}, "right": {"name": "The firewall", "note": "Filters network traffic"}, "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "A reset hands over the account. No firewall stops a legitimate login."}, {"kind": "multipleChoice", "id": "mc", "prompt": "What cut the MGM attackers' path short at other firms?", "options": ["A longer password policy", "Verifying callers before any reset", "A faster internet connection"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "If the help desk verifies identity properly, a convincing voice is not enough."}, {"id": "lens-curiosity", "kind": "info", "title": "A call that cost millions", "body": "Attackers claimed a 10-minute call to a help desk opened the door to a $100 million hit.", "eyebrow": "Why it matters", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.adaptivesecurity.com/blog/mgm-ransomware-attack", "label": "Adaptive Security, the MGM attack, citing MGM filings"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In cyber, whoever can reset access holds the real power.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.adaptivesecurity.com/blog/mgm-ransomware-attack", "label": "Adaptive Security, the MGM attack, citing MGM filings"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "The 2023 MGM attack cost about $100 million. Attackers claimed a call to the IT help desk got them in, showing the power of whoever can reset access.", "sectionKey": "players"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$df6d4c22-f591-5ffc-8944-c80cc90a3a1b$ml$::uuid, $ml$cybersecurity$ml$, $ml$99.22%$ml$, 'MICRO_LESSON', ARRAY[$ml$MICRO_LESSON$ml$, $ml$day-22$ml$, $ml$month-1$ml$, $ml$handwritten-v1$ml$, $ml$goal:career$ml$, $ml$level:beginner$ml$, $ml$level:intermediate$ml$, $ml$level:advanced$ml$]::text[], 5, now(), '{}'::jsonb, $ml${"id": "cybersecurity-d22-career", "title": "99.22%", "exercises": [{"id": "open", "kind": "coldOpen", "kicker": "One setting, almost all the risk.", "eyebrow": "Day 22 · The numbers", "headline": "Microsoft researchers found MFA reduces the risk of account compromise by 99.22%.", "fullText": "They studied a large set of Microsoft Azure Active Directory users who showed suspicious activity. Even for accounts whose passwords had already leaked, MFA cut the risk of compromise by 98.56%. Few security controls move a number that far.", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.00945", "label": "Microsoft researchers, how effective is MFA"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "leaked", "eyebrow": "Even after a leak", "text": "A leaked password alone was usually not enough with MFA on.", "highlight": "Risk fell by 98.56% for those accounts.", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.00945", "label": "Microsoft researchers, how effective is MFA"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "cost", "eyebrow": "The trade", "text": "MFA adds a few seconds per login.", "highlight": "In exchange it removes almost all account takeover risk.", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.00945", "label": "Microsoft researchers, how effective is MFA"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "not-all", "eyebrow": "Read it right", "title": "Not all MFA is equal", "body": "Codes and push prompts can still be phished or approved by mistake. Stronger forms resist that.", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.00945", "label": "Microsoft researchers, how effective is MFA"}, {"url": "https://www.whitehouse.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/M-22-09.pdf", "label": "OMB memo M-22-09, federal zero trust strategy"}]}, {"kind": "numberSense", "id": "ns-mfa", "prompt": "By about what percent did MFA cut the risk of account compromise overall?", "min": 50, "max": 100, "value": 99, "unit": "%", "tolerance": 0.04, "explanation": "99.22% overall, and 98.56% even for accounts with leaked passwords."}, {"kind": "spotFake", "id": "fake", "prompt": "One of these is false. Tap it.", "statements": ["MFA cut compromise risk by 99.22% overall.", "MFA stopped working once a password leaked.", "The study used Azure Active Directory data."], "fakeIndex": 1, "explanation": "Even with leaked passwords, MFA cut risk by 98.56%."}, {"id": "lens-career", "kind": "info", "title": "Quote the MFA number", "body": "99.22% fewer compromises with MFA. It is the strongest single argument in account security.", "eyebrow": "For your interview", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.00945", "label": "Microsoft researchers, how effective is MFA"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In cyber, the cheapest controls are often the ones that matter most.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.00945", "label": "Microsoft researchers, how effective is MFA"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "Microsoft researchers found MFA cuts the risk of account compromise by 99.22%, and by 98.56% even when passwords have leaked.", "sectionKey": "numbers"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
