 M-22-09, federal zero trust strategy"}]}, {"kind": "numberSense", "id": "ns-mfa", "prompt": "By about what percent did MFA cut the risk of account compromise overall?", "min": 50, "max": 100, "value": 99, "unit": "%", "tolerance": 0.04, "explanation": "99.22% overall, and 98.56% even for accounts with leaked passwords."}, {"kind": "spotFake", "id": "fake", "prompt": "One of these is false. Tap it.", "statements": ["MFA cut compromise risk by 99.22% overall.", "MFA stopped working once a password leaked.", "The study used Azure Active Directory data."], "fakeIndex": 1, "explanation": "Even with leaked passwords, MFA cut risk by 98.56%."}, {"id": "lens-invest", "kind": "info", "title": "Simple controls, big effect", "body": "MFA removes most account takeover risk. Products that make it painless have a strong case.", "eyebrow": "For investors", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.00945", "label": "Microsoft researchers, how effective is MFA"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In cyber, the cheapest controls are often the ones that matter most.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.00945", "label": "Microsoft researchers, how effective is MFA"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "Microsoft researchers found MFA cuts the risk of account compromise by 99.22%, and by 98.56% even when passwords have leaked.", "sectionKey": "numbers"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
