($ml$920bb7ac-7acb-5c39-aacb-61aea5622670$ml$::uuid, $ml$7112bdec-5758-5b14-b112-dc1fd9136f8c$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$Staff, contractors and partners$ml$, $ml$Phishing-resistant MFA is required for agency staff, contractors and partners using enterprise-managed identities.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.whitehouse.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/M-22-09.pdf", "label": "OMB memo M-22-09, federal zero trust strategy"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$4ac20a0f-6e8c-54dd-a1a9-0bbc462ab77a$ml$::uuid, $ml$7112bdec-5758-5b14-b112-dc1fd9136f8c$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Build for the new bar$ml$, $ml$Government set phishing resistance as the standard. Products that meet it sell to agencies and beyond.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.whitehouse.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/M-22-09.pdf", "label": "OMB memo M-22-09, federal zero trust strategy"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$34630b59-6639-5fe5-8301-4fffb37534bc$ml$::uuid, $ml$7112bdec-5758-5b14-b112-dc1fd9136f8c$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In identity security, a login method is only as strong as the easiest way to trick it.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.whitehouse.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/M-22-09.pdf", "label": "OMB memo M-22-09, federal zero trust strategy"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$47a7335a-6d3f-59ca-a110-52782699a102$ml$::uuid, $ml$1ec5acc0-933c-5d9a-b054-ec0c2de364fa$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 23 · The rule$ml$, $ml$A January 2022 White House memo required phishing-resistant MFA for federal staff by the end of fiscal 2024. Memo M-22-09 told agencies to stop supporting login methods that fail to resist phishing, including SMS or voice codes, one-time codes and push notifications, for routine access. It also ordered them to drop password rules requiring special characters and regular rotation.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.whitehouse.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/M-22-09.pdf", "label": "OMB memo M-22-09, federal zero trust strategy"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$b2a6c040-d84c-5b73-a87f-2edcc8f21ac4$ml$::uuid, $ml$1ec5acc0-933c-5d9a-b054-ec0c2de364fa$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$The problem$ml$, $ml$A code can be typed into a fake site as easily as a real one. Phishing-resistant methods check the site, not just the person.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.whitehouse.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/M-22-09.pdf", "label": "OMB memo M-22-09, federal zero trust strategy"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$8a8d55a7-6306-5c98-97a3-b901f713a969$ml$::uuid, $ml$1ec5acc0-933c-5d9a-b054-ec0c2de364fa$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$The surprise$ml$, $ml$The memo dropped forced password changes and special-character rules. Agencies had one year to remove them.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.whitehouse.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/M-22-09.pdf", "label": "OMB memo M-22-09, federal zero trust strategy"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$7369045d-09fe-5ba2-8d7a-569dc7ea33d7$ml$::uuid, $ml$1ec5acc0-933c-5d9a-b054-ec0c2de364fa$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$Staff, contractors and partners$ml$, $ml$Phishing-resistant MFA is required for agency staff, contractors and partners using enterprise-managed identities.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.whitehouse.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/M-22-09.pdf", "label": "OMB memo M-22-09, federal zero trust strategy"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$fc5dddbe-3f60-510e-9398-e9efe2aad5af$ml$::uuid, $ml$1ec5acc0-933c-5d9a-b054-ec0c2de364fa$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Goodbye forced password changes$ml$, $ml$A US government memo told agencies to stop forcing regular password changes and special characters.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.whitehouse.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/M-22-09.pdf", "label": "OMB memo M-22-09, federal zero trust strategy"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$3be7fca3-c251-50ea-a8b4-6fa13f1dfa6a$ml$::uuid, $ml$1ec5acc0-933c-5d9a-b054-ec0c2de364fa$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In identity security, a login method is only as strong as the easiest way to trick it.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.whitehouse.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/M-22-09.pdf", "label": "OMB memo M-22-09, federal zero trust strategy"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$18314eb6-658a-5f1e-8e1c-d6137666b845$ml$::uuid, $ml$7a98fc26-a5ca-5a19-a23a-21139b0e2eeb$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 24 · Where it breaks$ml$, $ml$By December 2024, Google said 800 million accounts used passkeys, and Amazon users had created 175 million. A passkey lets you sign in with your device, often with a fingerprint or face scan, instead of a password, and it resists phishing. Microsoft said passkey sign-ins were three times faster than passwords and succeeded 98% of the time, against 32% for passwords. It aims to remove passwords completely.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.biometricupdate.com/202412/passkeys-build-momentum-enabling-access-to-15-billion-online-accounts", "label": "Biometric Update, passkeys build momentum"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$dd6a6df0-03d3-56c1-ac58-19f2af8fa0f3$ml$::uuid, $ml$7a98fc26-a5ca-5a19-a23a-21139b0e2eeb$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$How it works$ml$, $ml$A passkey is tied to your device and to the real website. A fake site cannot collect it the way it collects a password.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.biometricupdate.com/202412/passkeys-build-momentum-enabling-access-to-15-billion-online-accounts", "label": "Biometric Update, passkeys build momentum"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$ada7ed2e-137d-5eb3-ac9b-11a3083ef2e1$ml$::uuid, $ml$7a98fc26-a5ca-5a19-a23a-21139b0e2eeb$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$The numbers$ml$, $ml$Microsoft saw 98% of passkey sign-ins succeed, versus 32% for passwords. Fewer failed logins also means fewer help desk calls.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.biometricupdate.com/202412/passkeys-build-momentum-enabling-access-to-15-billion-online-accounts", "label": "Biometric Update, passkeys build momentum"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$094ed4bf-9115-5cc3-8f36-6ac58c0cdf73$ml$::uuid, $ml$7a98fc26-a5ca-5a19-a23a-21139b0e2eeb$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$Adoption is still early$ml$, $ml$Big platforms support passkeys, but most companies still run on passwords. Moving everyone over is the open problem.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.biometricupdate.com/202412/passkeys-build-momentum-enabling-access-to-15-billion-online-accounts", "label": "Biometric Update, passkeys build momentum"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$77119d43-3fb8-5ea4-9a1d-cee697fb13a0$ml$::uuid, $ml$7a98fc26-a5ca-5a19-a23a-21139b0e2eeb$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Know where identity is heading$ml$, $ml$Passkeys are replacing passwords at Google, Amazon and Microsoft. Knowing how they work puts you ahead.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.biometricupdate.com/202412/passkeys-build-momentum-enabling-access-to-15-billion-online-accounts", "label": "Biometric Update, passkeys build momentum"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$4dc22924-d77c-53dd-bbff-b403c5c017f5$ml$::uuid, $ml$7a98fc26-a5ca-5a19-a23a-21139b0e2eeb$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In identity, the safest password may be no password at all.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.biometricupdate.com/202412/passkeys-build-momentum-enabling-access-to-15-billion-online-accounts", "label": "Biometric Update, passkeys build momentum"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$7bef4d28-7128-549a-832d-a77936ba6e6f$ml$::uuid, $ml$155bd252-26bb-51d5-906d-ce667bedd803$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 24 · Where it breaks$ml$, $ml$By December 2024, Google said 800 million accounts used passkeys, and Amazon users had created 175 million. A passkey lets you sign in with your device, often with a fingerprint or face scan, instead of a password, and it resists phishing. Microsoft said passkey sign-ins were three times faster than passwords and succeeded 98% of the time, against 32% for passwords. It aims to remove passwords completely.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.biometricupdate.com/202412/passkeys-build-momentum-enabling-access-to-15-billion-online-accounts", "label": "Biometric Update, passkeys build momentum"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$76ddfc55-2950-5ac2-84fd-7fbd4fa6dbef$ml$::uuid, $ml$155bd252-26bb-51d5-906d-ce667bedd803$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$How it works$ml$, $ml$A passkey is tied to your device and to the real website. A fake site cannot collect it the way it collects a password.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.biometricupdate.com/202412/passkeys-build-momentum-enabling-access-to-15-billion-online-accounts", "label": "Biometric Update, passkeys build momentum"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$16d30f85-aebe-5253-80cf-4354b8780424$ml$::uuid, $ml$155bd252-26bb-51d5-906d-ce667bedd803$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$The numbers$ml$, $ml$Microsoft saw 98% of passkey sign-ins succeed, versus 32% for passwords. Fewer failed logins also means fewer help desk calls.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.biometricupdate.com/202412/passkeys-build-momentum-enabling-access-to-15-billion-online-accounts", "label": "Biometric Update, passkeys build momentum"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$19083cd0-dd09-5e4f-8b49-5f9c9211abc6$ml$::uuid, $ml$155bd252-26bb-51d5-906d-ce667bedd803$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$Adoption is still early$ml$, $ml$Big platforms support passkeys, but most companies still run on passwords. Moving everyone over is the open problem.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.biometricupdate.com/202412/passkeys-build-momentum-enabling-access-to-15-billion-online-accounts", "label": "Biometric Update, passkeys build momentum"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$f0e5963f-962c-500e-b5a8-c251c2a937f9$ml$::uuid, $ml$155bd252-26bb-51d5-906d-ce667bedd803$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$The password economy shrinks$ml$, $ml$If passwords fade, resets and phishing losses shrink with them. Watch who sells the replacement.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.biometricupdate.com/202412/passkeys-build-momentum-enabling-access-to-15-billion-online-accounts", "label": "Biometric Update, passkeys build momentum"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$ef54fb6b-dd18-5622-8cfc-83ea781ac2b0$ml$::uuid, $ml$155bd252-26bb-51d5-906d-ce667bedd803$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In identity, the safest password may be no password at all.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.biometricupdate.com/202412/passkeys-build-momentum-enabling-access-to-15-billion-online-accounts", "label": "Biometric Update, passkeys build momentum"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$a113eec1-31b4-5537-8597-78559636bf34$ml$::uuid, $ml$b66e7d5e-fc62-518e-86a0-63a98b99e08f$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 24 · Where it breaks$ml$, $ml$By December 2024, Google said 800 million accounts used passkeys, and Amazon users had created 175 million. A passkey lets you sign in with your device, often with a fingerprint or face scan, instead of a password, and it resists phishing. Microsoft said passkey sign-ins were three times faster than passwords and succeeded 98% of the time, against 32% for passwords. It aims to remove passwords completely.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.biometricupdate.com/202412/passkeys-build-momentum-enabling-access-to-15-billion-online-accounts", "label": "Biometric Update, passkeys build momentum"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$90d6a0c1-41f4-5bd1-92e1-02b0f43e0737$ml$::uuid, $ml$b66e7d5e-fc62-518e-86a0-63a98b99e08f$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$How it works$ml$, $ml$A passkey is tied to your device and to the real website. A fake site cannot collect it the way it collects a password.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.biometricupdate.com/202412/passkeys-build-momentum-enabling-access-to-15-billion-online-accounts", "label": "Biometric Update, passkeys build momentum"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$80ff3451-fd2d-599a-9eb7-f7025abc19b6$ml$::uuid, $ml$b66e7d5e-fc62-518e-86a0-63a98b99e08f$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$The numbers$ml$, $ml$Microsoft saw 98% of passkey sign-ins succeed, versus 32% for passwords. Fewer failed logins also means fewer help desk calls.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.biometricupdate.com/202412/passkeys-build-momentum-enabling-access-to-15-billion-online-accounts", "label": "Biometric Update, passkeys build momentum"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$57a63d3f-29c8-5861-9f50-35b4882072ed$ml$::uuid, $ml$b66e7d5e-fc62-518e-86a0-63a98b99e08f$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$Adoption is still early$ml$, $ml$Big platforms support passkeys, but most companies still run on passwords. Moving everyone over is the open problem.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.biometricupdate.com/202412/passkeys-build-momentum-enabling-access-to-15-billion-online-accounts", "label": "Biometric Update, passkeys build momentum"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$5ac21a59-8fd0-5b34-84c9-96905b48074f$ml$::uuid, $ml$b66e7d5e-fc62-518e-86a0-63a98b99e08f$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Help everyone else switch$ml$, $ml$Big platforms have passkeys; most companies do not. Making the switch easy is the opening.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.biometricupdate.com/202412/passkeys-build-momentum-enabling-access-to-15-billion-online-accounts", "label": "Biometric Update, passkeys build momentum"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$6bf5dd5d-53ed-56a4-b76f-3dda88dbbc7c$ml$::uuid, $ml$b66e7d5e-fc62-518e-86a0-63a98b99e08f$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In identity, the safest password may be no password at all.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.biometricupdate.com/202412/passkeys-build-momentum-enabling-access-to-15-billion-online-accounts", "label": "Biometric Update, passkeys build momentum"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$0c2bb850-e109-55de-807c-503267bec274$ml$::uuid, $ml$cdc8ea2b-8c32-55e2-9e34-dfb05c0c4e27$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 24 · Where it breaks$ml$, $ml$By December 2024, Google said 800 million accounts used passkeys, and Amazon users had created 175 million. A passkey lets you sign in with your device, often with a fingerprint or face scan, instead of a password, and it resists phishing. Microsoft said passkey sign-ins were three times faster than passwords and succeeded 98% of the time, against 32% for passwords. It aims to remove passwords completely.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.biometricupdate.com/202412/passkeys-build-momentum-enabling-access-to-15-billion-online-accounts", "label": "Biometric Update, passkeys build momentum"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$75748ef2-2601-5666-ba60-b8b742481940$ml$::uuid, $ml$cdc8ea2b-8c32-55e2-9e34-dfb05c0c4e27$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$How it works$ml$, $ml$A passkey is tied to your device and to the real website. A fake site cannot collect it the way it collects a password.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.biometricupdate.com/202412/passkeys-build-momentum-enabling-access-to-15-billion-online-accounts", "label": "Biometric Update, passkeys build momentum"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$23600da5-1adc-5c32-863d-8ed913a49dc2$ml$::uuid, $ml$cdc8ea2b-8c32-55e2-9e34-dfb05c0c4e27$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$The numbers$ml$, $ml$Microsoft saw 98% of passkey sign-ins succeed, versus 32% for passwords. Fewer failed logins also means fewer help desk calls.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.biometricupdate.com/202412/passkeys-build-momentum-enabling-access-to-15-billion-online-accounts", "label": "Biometric Update, passkeys build momentum"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$afa5cd51-7112-53e2-b912-e97f254924fd$ml$::uuid, $ml$cdc8ea2b-8c32-55e2-9e34-dfb05c0c4e27$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$Adoption is still early$ml$, $ml$Big platforms support passkeys, but most companies still run on passwords. Moving everyone over is the open problem.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.biometricupdate.com/202412/passkeys-build-momentum-enabling-access-to-15-billion-online-accounts", "label": "Biometric Update, passkeys build momentum"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$14ee9b1c-876a-5bd8-915c-c947577c9d60$ml$::uuid, $ml$cdc8ea2b-8c32-55e2-9e34-dfb05c0c4e27$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Logging in with your face$ml$, $ml$800 million Google accounts used passkeys by late 2024, signing in with a device instead of a password.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.biometricupdate.com/202412/passkeys-build-momentum-enabling-access-to-15-billion-online-accounts", "label": "Biometric Update, passkeys build momentum"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$6bf72408-19ed-5316-8984-38c065aee323$ml$::uuid, $ml$cdc8ea2b-8c32-55e2-9e34-dfb05c0c4e27$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In identity, the safest password may be no password at all.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.biometricupdate.com/202412/passkeys-build-momentum-enabling-access-to-15-billion-online-accounts", "label": "Biometric Update, passkeys build momentum"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$ea5c8391-8255-5dd5-b8f4-081c1b2a5e85$ml$::uuid, $ml$36e8a1af-828c-5240-ac21-c7ea057a3e8d$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 25 · Zero trust$ml$, $ml$In August 2020, NIST defined zero trust: no implicit trust based on where a user or device sits on the network. The old model was a castle: a strong wall, with everyone inside trusted. Zero trust moves defenses from the network perimeter to users, assets and resources. Every user and device is authenticated and authorized before each session, wherever it connects from.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://csrc.nist.gov/Pubs/sp/800/207/Final", "label": "NIST SP 800-207, Zero Trust Architecture"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$181a738c-c73a-5ebf-b0da-5e3743dc5ec2$ml$::uuid, $ml$36e8a1af-828c-5240-ac21-c7ea057a3e8d$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$The old model$ml$, $ml$Perimeter security trusts anyone already inside the wall. One stolen login could open the whole network.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://csrc.nist.gov/Pubs/sp/800/207/Final", "label": "NIST SP 800-207, Zero Trust Architecture"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$b1b34ff9-b4de-511b-a935-eccfb5fe411a$ml$::uuid, $ml$36e8a1af-828c-5240-ac21-c7ea057a3e8d$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$The new model$ml$, $ml$Zero trust checks the user and the device before every session. Location alone earns nothing.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://csrc.nist.gov/Pubs/sp/800/207/Final", "label": "NIST SP 800-207, Zero Trust Architecture"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$51bad302-8624-53df-a1e0-758be5a2fb36$ml$::uuid, $ml$36e8a1af-828c-5240-ac21-c7ea057a3e8d$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$The US government adopted it$ml$, $ml$A 2022 White House memo set zero trust goals for federal agencies to meet by the end of fiscal 2024.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.whitehouse.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/M-22-09.pdf", "label": "OMB memo M-22-09, federal zero trust strategy"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$fae5c8e4-ce44-5b19-aed7-84e779c8d318$ml$::uuid, $ml$36e8a1af-828c-5240-ac21-c7ea057a3e8d$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Define zero trust exactly$ml$, $ml$Say it like NIST: no implicit trust based on network location. Buzzword answers are easy to spot.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://csrc.nist.gov/Pubs/sp/800/207/Final", "label": "NIST SP 800-207, Zero Trust Architecture"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$4baea483-0c54-589a-bfa8-f7676592efac$ml$::uuid, $ml$36e8a1af-828c-5240-ac21-c7ea057a3e8d$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In network security, location is not identity.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://csrc.nist.gov/Pubs/sp/800/207/Final", "label": "NIST SP 800-207, Zero Trust Architecture"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$1bacf106-dd6f-5ca8-805f-21e2265c66ff$ml$::uuid, $ml$0be06cdd-6f5b-51e4-826c-e6c35db4f021$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 25 · Zero trust$ml$, $ml$In August 2020, NIST defined zero trust: no implicit trust based on where a user or device sits on the network. The old model was a castle: a strong wall, with everyone inside trusted. Zero trust moves defenses from the network perimeter to users, assets and resources. Every user and device is authenticated and authorized before each session, wherever it connects from.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://csrc.nist.gov/Pubs/sp/800/207/Final", "label": "NIST SP 800-207, Zero Trust Architecture"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$0ae0ea3b-edf9-5de9-855d-2f7a33793bc1$ml$::uuid, $ml$0be06cdd-6f5b-51e4-826c-e6c35db4f021$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$The old model$ml$, $ml$Perimeter security trusts anyone already inside the wall. One stolen login could open the whole network.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://csrc.nist.gov/Pubs/sp/800/207/Final", "label": "NIST SP 800-207, Zero Trust Architecture"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$539e6bcc-0e9c-5cd0-9c6c-c57386257b8f$ml$::uuid, $ml$0be06cdd-6f5b-51e4-826c-e6c35db4f021$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$The new model$ml$, $ml$Zero trust checks the user and the device before every session. Location alone earns nothing.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://csrc.nist.gov/Pubs/sp/800/207/Final", "label": "NIST SP 800-207, Zero Trust Architecture"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$9622c8ed-6840-53c4-8086-9db5541b2919$ml$::uuid, $ml$0be06cdd-6f5b-51e4-826c-e6c35db4f021$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$The US government adopted it$ml$, $ml$A 2022 White House memo set zero trust goals for federal agencies to meet by the end of fiscal 2024.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.whitehouse.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/M-22-09.pdf", "label": "OMB memo M-22-09, federal zero trust strategy"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$c27cff77-567b-5baa-bc1a-75707a96c32c$ml$::uuid, $ml$0be06cdd-6f5b-51e4-826c-e6c35db4f021$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$A long migration$ml$, $ml$Moving from perimeters to zero trust takes years, which keeps spending going.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://csrc.nist.gov/Pubs/sp/800/207/Final", "label": "NIST SP 800-207, Zero Trust Architecture"}, {"url": "https://www.whitehouse.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/M-22-09.pdf", "label": "OMB memo M-22-09, federal zero trust strategy"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$785026ae-18a5-5dcf-9d75-ded16b7f0dd9$ml$::uuid, $ml$0be06cdd-6f5b-51e4-826c-e6c35db4f021$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In network security, location is not identity.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://csrc.nist.gov/Pubs/sp/800/207/Final", "label": "NIST SP 800-207, Zero Trust Architecture"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$48179371-f06d-536e-b77e-2c559a4aafc6$ml$::uuid, $ml$172a3281-2775-52d1-9254-521b1501f186$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 25 · Zero trust$ml$, $ml$In August 2020, NIST defined zero trust: no implicit trust based on where a user or device sits on the network. The old model was a castle: a strong wall, with everyone inside trusted. Zero trust moves defenses from the network perimeter to users, assets and resources. Every user and device is authenticated and authorized before each session, wherever it connects from.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://csrc.nist.gov/Pubs/sp/800/207/Final", "label": "NIST SP 800-207, Zero Trust Architecture"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$fe9434b0-f814-57cd-8596-94919db36079$ml$::uuid, $ml$172a3281-2775-52d1-9254-521b1501f186$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$The old model$ml$, $ml$Perimeter security trusts anyone already inside the wall. One stolen login could open the whole network.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://csrc.nist.gov/Pubs/sp/800/207/Final", "label": "NIST SP 800-207, Zero Trust Architecture"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$847a8fbf-7485-58e8-ae31-13bcd1d79352$ml$::uuid, $ml$172a3281-2775-52d1-9254-521b1501f186$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$The new model$ml$, $ml$Zero trust checks the user and the device before every session. Location alone earns nothing.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://csrc.nist.gov/Pubs/sp/800/207/Final", "label": "NIST SP 800-207, Zero Trust Architecture"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$5f18a61e-d422-5600-be97-8b670a8311df$ml$::uuid, $ml$172a3281-2775-52d1-9254-521b1501f186$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$The US government adopted it$ml$, $ml$A 2022 White House memo set zero trust goals for federal agencies to meet by the end of fiscal 2024.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.whitehouse.gov/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/M-22-09.pdf", "label": "OMB memo M-22-09, federal zero trust strategy"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$bfdd37a9-2c06-5074-a261-1d815d8f4054$ml$::uuid, $ml$172a3281-2775-52d1-9254-521b1501f186$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Help with the shift$ml$, $ml$Most companies still run perimeter networks. Tools that ease the move to zero trust have buyers.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://csrc.nist.gov/Pubs/sp/800/207/Final", "label": "NIST SP 800-207, Zero Trust Architecture"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$af794d47-4982-5937-b7ea-0991de3fb7f9$ml$::uuid, $ml$172a3281-2775-52d1-9254-521b1501f186$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In network security, location is not identity.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://csrc.nist.gov/Pubs/sp/800/207/Final", "label": "NIST SP 800-207, Zero Trust Architecture"}]$ml$::jsonb),
